# Input graph for isamplesorg__vocabularies__tools_navocab___init__.py__VocabularyStore_broader
# Covers: several solutions of the v=None pattern (Dog has two broader
# concepts: Animal and Mammal), the v=<scheme> pattern narrowing that set
# down to one (Mammal is in a different scheme and must be excluded), the
# zero-solution case (Rock has no broader concepts at all), and neighbouring
# triples that must NOT match: Cat is broader-than Animal but is not Dog,
# and Fish is skos:related (not skos:broader) to Animal.
@prefix ex:   <http://example.org/> .
@prefix skos: <http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#> .

ex:Dog    a skos:Concept ; skos:broader ex:Animal ; skos:inScheme ex:VocabA .
ex:Dog    skos:broader ex:Mammal .
ex:Mammal a skos:Concept ; skos:inScheme ex:VocabB .
ex:Animal a skos:Concept ; skos:inScheme ex:VocabA .
ex:Rock   a skos:Concept ; skos:inScheme ex:VocabA .

# neighbourhood that must NOT match a broader(Dog) query
ex:Cat    a skos:Concept ; skos:broader ex:Animal ; skos:inScheme ex:VocabA .
ex:Fish   a skos:Concept ; skos:related ex:Animal ; skos:inScheme ex:VocabA .

# Added by the audit of 2026-10-31 (see meta.json "repair"): a third broader
# concept of Dog whose skos:inScheme is a LITERAL carrying the VocabA IRI's
# text.  On the in-memory graph an IRI binding of ?vocabulary never reaches
# it; on the store the repository really uses (rdflib-sqlalchemy, emulated in
# driver.py) a non-Literal binding is matched by text in the literal
# partition too, so the original returns Canid there -- and so must the
# translation.  A Literal binding would match Canid ONLY, and lose Animal.
ex:Dog    skos:broader ex:Canid .
ex:Canid  a skos:Concept ; skos:inScheme "http://example.org/VocabA" .
